== the poems you never showed anyone
tier: echo
rate: 0.05
once: yes
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 25-100
times: per adult who wrote poems, once: about 1 in 10 return to writing after a loss or in retirement (estimate)
tone: mixed
life: writing, grief, meaning
horizon: months
roles: partner, child, friend, the host of the poetry night
earth: after a loss or a retirement, the notebook of poems in a drawer, never shown to anyone
tribal: after a loss or the end of the hunting years, the verses made alone and never spoken at the fire
magic: after a loss or the end of working days, the bundle of verses tied with string in a drawer
after: moment 'the first months of retirement', 'your partner dies' or 'the notebooks come together' in someone who writes ([writing poems] or [writing stories]), 25 or more, who neither reads at a poetry night nor writes books (no [open-mic poet], no [novelist])
delay: 0-1
likelier: much Red and White in the colour history; time to spare
rarer: stress above 1.8; poor health
scene: After the loss, or the last day at work, the days are suddenly wide and quiet. At the back of a drawer {N} finds the notebook: thirty years of poems, written late at night, on trains and in waiting rooms, and never shown to a single person.
scene W: {N} wrote them the way some people keep a diary, faithfully, one a week for years, and then stopped. It seems a kind of duty to see them through to something.
scene U: Reading them now, {N} can see what each one was trying to do, where it fell short, and how it could be mended. The old ones read like drafts of something not yet written.
scene B: A few of them are good, better than some {N} has seen in print. It is strange to have kept them hidden for so long, out of nothing but fear.
scene R: {N} reads the first one and has to sit down on the floor. Every feeling of thirty years is in there, as raw as the day it was written.
scene G: The poems follow the years like rings in a tree: the first flat, the children small, the garden, the long illness, the seasons going round. They belong to the family as much as to {N}.
scene tribal: In the quiet after a loss, or when the hunt goes on without {N}, the old verses come back: made alone on long walks, never once spoken at the fire. {N} still knows every one of them.
scene magic: After a loss, or the last day in the counting house, {N} finds the bundle of verses tied with string at the back of a drawer: thirty years of them, written by candlelight and on the road, and never read by anyone.
- W | read one at the library's poetry evening, and go back every month after | v: conformity, tradition | title: open-mic poet | tribal: speak one at the fire on the next long night, and stand again every long night after | magic: read one at the poets' night in the tavern, and go back every month after | chance: 70
- U | type them up, sort them, and redraft the best twenty into a collection | v: achievement, self-direction | aims: novelist | identity | tribal: shape the best of them into one long telling, verse by verse, until it holds together | magic: copy them out fair, sort them, and redraft the best twenty into a book | chance: 70
